;;; A rule whose `:match` is `()` never fired — regression
;;; `empty-match-fact` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(q a)` and a rule with no premise that concludes `(q b)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An empty conjunction is true, so the rule fires once (Q-M1e2.36): root
;;; holds `(q b :rule r)` beside `(q a)`, and R's minimal line is root's state,
;;; `(model (q a) (q b))`.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The rule loaded and never fired (`ein saturate FILE --dump`): root held
;;; `(q a)` alone, `(q b)` was a candidate, and R eight states, exit 0 (`ein
;;; solve FILE` and `ein oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s,
;;; release and debug alike).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `:match ()` is the empty conjunction, so `r` fires once at root: root
;;; holds `(q b :rule r)` beside `(q a)`, H is `(p a)` and `(p b)`, and R
;;; their cube, its minimal line root's state, the answer above by hand. The
;;; regression's `now` is that answer; the section above is the record of the
;;; one it replaced.
;;;
;;;

(relation q Node :why "q holds of {?1}") (relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}")
(q a)
(rule r () :match () :assert (q b) :why "the rule has no premise, so q holds of b")
